 `EdgeMeshGameServers.md` phase 2, connection 3: authenticated sessions.
 Connection 1 answers where a match can be placed; this answers who is
 allowed to be told.

 IDENTITY IS CHECKED BEFORE PLACEMENT AND THE ORDER IS THE POINT. An
 unauthenticated caller gets the same refusal whether the region is busy,
 empty, or not a region at all, so nothing about capacity or topology leaks
 to someone who has not proved who they are. Reversing these two lines would
 leave the refusal text as a free map of the mesh.

 A peer that exists is not a peer that is authenticated. `node-find-peer`
 answers the first question and `trust-is-authenticated` the second, and only
 the second one decides.

Section: Admission

  MeshAdmission =
    | Admitted (Text)
    | AdmissionRefused (Text)

  mesh-peer-authenticated : TrustNode, Text -> Boolean
  mesh-peer-authenticated (node) (peer) =
    when node-find-peer node peer
      is None -> False
      is Just (i) -> trust-is-authenticated ((node-get-peer node i).session)

 One refusal text for both identity failures. An unknown peer and a known
 but unauthenticated one are the same answer on purpose: telling them apart
 tells an attacker which names exist.

  mesh-admit : TrustNode, GroupState, RegionList, Integer, Text -> MeshAdmission
  mesh-admit (node) (g) (rl) (region-idx) (peer) =
    if mesh-peer-authenticated node peer == False
      then AdmissionRefused "no authenticated session"
    else when mesh-place g rl region-idx
      is PlacementRefused (why) -> AdmissionRefused why
      is PlacedAt (loc) -> Admitted (loc.sl-member-id)

Section: Reporting

  mesh-admission-label : MeshAdmission -> Text
  mesh-admission-label (a) =
    when a
      is Admitted (m) -> "admitted to " & m
      is AdmissionRefused (why) -> "refused: " & why
